// Concurrency + GC stress suite — the Bit port of `tests/stress.zig` (task #350,
// ported under #1593's retire-the-seed programme). Each subdirectory of
// `tests/stress/` is a Bit program that hammers spawn / channels / select / the
// collector and prints one deterministic line encoding its own correctness. Each
// is compiled, then run TWICE: once under the default collector policy and once
// under `BIT_GC=stress`, which collects at every safepoint. The stress run is a
// precise-rooting oracle — any root the compiler or runtime fails to report is
// swept the instant it stops being marked, so a rooting bug that a rare
// production collection would only occasionally hit becomes a deterministic
// wrong answer here. Both runs must reproduce the program's `.expected` stdout.
//
// THE SEED PASS IS GONE, NOT PORTED, AND THAT IS THE ONE THING TO NOTICE.
// `tests/stress.zig` built every program with BOTH compilers — `bit-seed`
// in-process and self-hosted `bit` as a subprocess — and its header carried a
// paragraph (#1413) about how the self-hosted pass being absent had once read as
// coverage. This port drives the SELF-HOSTED COMPILER ONLY. That is deliberate:
// the seed is the retiring bootstrap oracle, `bit` is what ships, and a harness
// written in Bit cannot import a Zig module in-process anyway. But #1413's
// lesson runs both ways — a dropped pass that nobody writes down reads as
// coverage exactly like a missing one did. It is dropped, it is stated here, and
// it belongs on #1593's ledger.
//
// THE ORACLE IS ONLY AS DENSE AS THE SAFEPOINTS, and a program must earn it.
// Codegen emits `bit_rt_safepoint` at loop back-edges (runtime/ABI.md §4);
// `bit_rt_alloc` does NOT poll, so allocating does not by itself collect. A
// straight-line program therefore runs ZERO collections under `BIT_GC=stress` no
// matter how much it allocates, and its second run verifies nothing about
// rooting — it is the same run twice. #1438 made that rule enforced rather than
// merely stated: every stress run sets `BIT_GC_STATS=1` and must show a non-zero
// collection count, and a legitimately straight-line program declares itself
// with a non-empty `no-collect` file saying why.
//
// AND THE POLICY IS NOW PROVEN TO HAVE ARRIVED, WHICH #1438's OWN GUARD COULD
// NOT DO. `collections != 0` cannot tell a real `BIT_GC=stress` run from the
// default run repeated — the churn programs collect under the default policy
// too — so a harness whose env plumbing silently failed would pass #1438's check
// on every program while exercising precise rooting exactly zero times. The
// aggregate stats line does not name the active policy (that is the honest fix,
// and it needs `statsReport` in runtime/root/{linux,darwin}/boot.bit to emit
// `policy=<default|stress|off>`), so this port closes the hole differentially
// instead: BOTH runs carry `BIT_GC_STATS=1`, and a MAJORITY of the oracled
// programs must show a stress count more than `stressRatio` times their default
// count. Allocation-driven programs clear it by two to five orders of magnitude
// (`gcgrow`: 1 against 1,476,203). Clock-paced ones do not and must not be made
// to — `schedtimer` measures 99 against 41 — which is why the assertion is over
// the CORPUS, where it belongs: "the environment reached the child" is a fact
// about this harness, not about any one program. A `stress-waived` program gets
// the mirror proof directly: its second run is `BIT_GC=off`, which must collect
// EXACTLY zero.
//
// A MISSING STATS LINE IS FATAL, NEVER A SKIP. `osRunBounded` returns only an
// exit code, so stdout and stderr exist solely as files the wrapper redirected
// into — if the wrapper never execs, or a redirect is dropped, the reads come
// back empty and every field lookup "finds nothing". Treating that as "this
// build does not report the field" would switch the rooting oracle off for the
// whole corpus in one line with no error text anywhere. Absent is an error —
// with one DECLARED exception, `no-stats`, for a program that replaces the
// runtime's own write/exit provider and so never reaches `statsReport`
// (`rootiodarwin`). Declared, with a reason, and it costs that program the
// collection oracles while keeping its stdout comparison under both policies.
//
// A THIRD MARKER, AND IT IS THE WEAKEST THING IN THIS FILE — `stress-waived`.
// `no-collect` says "the collector cannot matter here"; `stress-waived` says
// "the collector DOES matter here and this program does not survive it yet". The
// second run switches to `BIT_GC=off`, so the program keeps proving its stdout
// under one policy and proves nothing about rooting under the other. The
// marker's whole design is that the hole cannot hide: the reason is mandatory,
// and it is printed on EVERY run. A waiver never suppresses the stdout
// comparison — only the policy changes.
//
// EVERY SUBPROCESS CARRIES A WALL-CLOCK DEADLINE. This is the corpus most able
// to hang, and a program that deadlocks must be reported as a failure naming
// itself rather than stalling the suite. `osRunBounded` (ABI.md §19) bounds the
// compile and both runs. `BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S` overrides the 300s default.
// CAVEAT, and it is a real divergence from `tests/proc.zig`: `BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S=0`
// meant "block forever" there, and `osRunBounded` has no unbounded mode — it
// clamps to one hour. `0` is therefore honored as ONE HOUR here, loudly, rather
// than silently redefined.
//
// WHY A `/bin/sh` WRAPPER. `osRunBounded(path, timeout_ms)` takes no argv, no
// environment and captures no output, so a shell script is the only way to hand
// a child `BIT_GC=stress` and keep its stdout. `exec` in the wrapper is
// LOAD-BEARING, not style: without it the shell stays as the child, and
// `osRunBounded`'s SIGKILL on timeout kills the shell and orphans the deadlocked
// stress binary — precisely the hazard this corpus exists to catch. The honest
// fix is an `osRunBoundedEnv(path, argv, env, timeout_ms)` primitive that returns
// captured pipes; until it exists this suite has a shell dependency it did not
// have in Zig.
//
// Run it: `zig-out/bin/bit run tests/bit/stress.bit`, from the repository root.
// `BIT_STRESSGATE_DIR` / `BIT_STRESSGATE_ONLY` narrow the run for debugging and
// mutation-testing; either one turns the corpus floors off and prints NOT A GATE.

// Upper bound on the directory walk (Power of 10 rule 2).
const maxPrograms: int = 256

// Corpus floors. A suite that is one loop over a directory reports "0 programs,
// 0 failures" and exits 0 when the walk finds nothing, so the counts are
// asserted rather than trusted. `minRunnable` is the per-host half: 38 of the 78
// directories carry a host marker, so a gate that skipped everything would still
// look busy.
const minPrograms: int = 78
const minRunnable: int = 59

// How many directories must be skipped for belonging to the OTHER host — 19
// `darwin-only` and 19 `linux-only` today, and the fact that those two numbers
// are EQUAL is why this constant exists. Inverting the host gate swaps which 19
// are skipped and leaves `minRunnable` satisfied at 59; mutation-testing the
// runnable floor is how that was found. Only a per-marker-kind assertion sees it.
const minHostSkips: int = 19

// How far above its default-policy count a program's stress-policy count must
// sit before the second run counts as having had a different policy. See the
// header: the measured margins are 250x and up.
const stressRatio: int = 8

// The corpus-wide plumbing assertion: at least 1-in-`minProofShare` of the
// oracled programs must clear `stressRatio`, and the check only applies once
// `minProofSample` of them have run (a one-program debugging run proves nothing
// about plumbing and should not pretend to).
const minProofShare: int = 2
const minProofSample: int = 4

const defaultTimeoutS: int = 300
const clampTimeoutMs: int = 3600000

const targetX64Linux: int = 0
const targetArm64Linux: int = 1
const targetArm64Macos: int = 2

const aggregatePrefix: string = "[bit-gc] collections="

struct Ctx {
  root: string,
  compiler: string,
  stdlib: string,
  libbitrt: string,
  scratch: string,
  timeoutMs: int,
  target: int,
}

struct Text { ok: bool, body: string }

struct Marker { present: bool, reason: string }

// One execution's verdict. `statsOk` is separate from `collections` for the
// reason the header gives: absent and zero are different answers.
struct Outcome { ok: bool, statsOk: bool, collections: int, abandoned: int }

// One program's verdict. `oracled` marks a program the collection oracle applies
// to at all; `proved` marks one whose stress count cleared `stressRatio`, which
// is what `main`'s corpus-wide plumbing assertion counts.
struct Verdict { failed: int, oracled: bool, proved: bool }

function say(s: string) {
  print(s)
  print("\n")
}

// Single-file mode has no prelude, so `string(i)` is not a conversion; the one
// int-to-string road is interpolation.
function num(v: int): string {
  return "${v}"
}

function die(s: string) {
  say("stress: FATAL: " + s)
  osExit(1)
}

// --- small string helpers (single-file mode has no prelude, no std/*) -------

function indexOfAt(hay: string, needle: string, at: int): int {
  let n = len(needle)
  if (n == 0) { return at }
  let i = at
  while (i + n <= len(hay)) {
    let j = 0
    while (j < n && hay[i + j] == needle[j]) { j = j + 1 }
    if (j == n) { return i }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return -1
}

function trim(s: string): string {
  let a = 0
  let b = len(s)
  while (a < b && (s[a] == 32 || s[a] == 9 || s[a] == 13 || s[a] == 10)) { a = a + 1 }
  while (b > a && (s[b - 1] == 32 || s[b - 1] == 9 || s[b - 1] == 13 || s[b - 1] == 10)) { b = b - 1 }
  return s[a:b]
}

function lessStr(a: string, b: string): bool {
  let n = len(a)
  if (len(b) < n) { n = len(b) }
  let i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    if (a[i] != b[i]) { return a[i] < b[i] }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return len(a) < len(b)
}

// `fsListDir` returns entries NUL-separated: NUL is the one byte a POSIX
// filename cannot contain, so unlike a newline it can never appear inside a name.
function splitNul(raw: string): []string {
  let out = []string(0)
  let start = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(raw)) {
    if (raw[i] == 0) {
      out = append(out, raw[start:i])
      start = i + 1
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return out
}

function sortNames(names: []string): []string {
  let i = 1
  while (i < len(names)) {
    let j = i
    while (j > 0 && lessStr(names[j], names[j - 1])) {
      let tmp = names[j - 1]
      names[j - 1] = names[j]
      names[j] = tmp
      j = j - 1
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return names
}

// POSIX single-quoting: everything is literal inside '...', and an embedded
// quote closes, escapes and reopens. The one correct way to put a path this
// harness did not choose into a shell command.
function shQuote(s: string): string {
  let out = "'"
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(s)) {
    if (s[i] == 39) { out = out + "'\\''" } else { out = out + s[i:i + 1] }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return out + "'"
}

// --- files ------------------------------------------------------------------

function readText(path: string): Text {
  let fd = fsOpen(path, false)
  if (fd < 0) { return Text{ ok: false, body: "" } }
  let body = fsReadAll(fd)
  fsClose(fd)
  return Text{ ok: true, body: body }
}

function writeText(path: string, body: string): bool {
  let fd = fsOpen(path, true)
  if (fd < 0) { return false }
  let n = fsWrite(fd, body)
  fsClose(fd)
  return n >= 0
}

// A per-directory oracle marker: absent, or the stated reason. An EMPTY marker
// is an error — a bare file would let a program weaken the oracle without anyone
// having to justify it, which is the failure mode the markers exist to prevent.
function readMarker(name: string, dir: string, marker: string): Marker {
  let t = readText(dir + "/" + marker)
  if (!t.ok) { return Marker{ present: false, reason: "" } }
  let reason = trim(t.body)
  if (len(reason) == 0) {
    die("stress '" + name + "': '" + marker + "' marker is empty - state why it applies")
  }
  return Marker{ present: true, reason: reason }
}

// --- the aggregate stats line ----------------------------------------------
//
// `runtime/root/{linux,darwin}/boot.bit`'s `statsReport` writes ONE summary line
// at exit under `BIT_GC_STATS`:
//   `[bit-gc] collections=<n> swept=<n> live=<n> abandoned=<n> oom=<n> bad=<n>`
// Every function on the collection path is `@nosplit`, which admits no
// print/extern/syscall (E0075), so there is no per-collection line to count.

function statsPresent(stderrText: string): bool {
  return indexOfAt(stderrText, aggregatePrefix, 0) >= 0
}

// `<field>=<n>` read from the aggregate line, or -1 when the field is absent.
// Callers must have checked `statsPresent` first: -1 here means malformed, and
// is never quietly folded into a count.
function statsField(stderrText: string, field: string): int {
  let line = indexOfAt(stderrText, aggregatePrefix, 0)
  if (line < 0) { return -1 }
  let key = indexOfAt(stderrText, field, line)
  if (key < 0) { return -1 }
  let i = key + len(field)
  if (i >= len(stderrText) || stderrText[i] < 48 || stderrText[i] > 57) { return -1 }
  let v = 0
  while (i < len(stderrText) && stderrText[i] >= 48 && stderrText[i] <= 57) {
    v = v * 10 + int(stderrText[i]) - 48
    i = i + 1
  }
  return v
}

// --- bounded subprocesses ---------------------------------------------------

// `osRunBounded`'s three outcomes in one i64 (ABI.md §19) rendered as a message,
// or "" when the child exited 0. Every branch names a DIFFERENT failure: a
// deadlock, an outside kill and a non-zero exit must not read alike.
function runMsg(r: int): string {
  if (r == 0) { return "" }
  if (r == -1) { return "SPAWN FAILED (fork/exec/wait error)" }
  if (r == -2) { return "TIMED OUT" }
  if (r <= -100) { return "KILLED BY SIGNAL " + num(0 - r - 100) }
  return "exited " + num(r)
}

// Write `body` as an executable `/bin/sh` script and run it under the deadline.
function runScript(cx: Ctx, path: string, body: string): int {
  if (!writeText(path, body)) { die("cannot write wrapper script " + path) }
  if (fsChmod(path, 493) != 0) { die("cannot chmod wrapper script " + path) }
  return osRunBounded(path, cx.timeoutMs)
}

// --- one program ------------------------------------------------------------

// Build `dir` with the self-hosted compiler. `BIT_LIBBITRT`/`BIT_STDLIB` pin the
// archive and stdlib so the harness is independent of its working directory, and
// a whole-PROJECT build (not a single module) is what `tests/stress/spinlock`
// needs: it imports the lock under test straight out of `runtime/`.
function buildProgram(cx: Ctx, name: string, dir: string, bin: string): bool {
  let base = cx.scratch + "/" + name
  let errPath = base + ".berr"
  let body = "#!/bin/sh\n" +
    "BIT_STDLIB=" + shQuote(cx.stdlib) +
    " BIT_LIBBITRT=" + shQuote(cx.libbitrt) +
    " exec " + shQuote(cx.compiler) + " build " + shQuote(dir) + " -o " + shQuote(bin) +
    " >" + shQuote(base + ".bout") + " 2>" + shQuote(errPath) + "\n"
  let r = runScript(cx, base + ".build.sh", body)
  if (r == 0) { return true }
  say("stress '" + name + "' [compile]: " + runMsg(r))
  let e = readText(errPath)
  if (e.ok) { say(trim(e.body)) }
  return false
}

// One execution. `envLine` is the environment prefix the wrapper applies; it is
// always the whole story about the policy, so a run is labelled by what it got.
//
// `slot` keeps the two runs' capture files APART, and that is not tidiness: with
// one shared pair of paths, a second run whose redirect never happened would
// read the FIRST run's output and its stats line, and report the default run's
// numbers as the stress run's. Absent has to look absent.
function runOnce(cx: Ctx, name: string, bin: string, slot: string, label: string, envLine: string, expected: string, statsRequired: bool): Outcome {
  let base = cx.scratch + "/" + name + "." + slot
  let outPath = base + ".out"
  let errPath = base + ".err"
  let body = "#!/bin/sh\nunset BIT_GC BIT_GC_STATS\n" +
    envLine + " exec " + shQuote(bin) +
    " >" + shQuote(outPath) + " 2>" + shQuote(errPath) + "\n"
  let r = runScript(cx, base + ".run.sh", body)

  let errText = readText(errPath)
  if (!errText.ok) {
    say("stress '" + name + "' [" + label + "]: could not read captured stderr " + errPath +
      " - the wrapper never ran, so nothing about this program was verified")
    return Outcome{ ok: false, statsOk: false, collections: -1, abandoned: -1 }
  }
  if (r != 0) {
    say("stress '" + name + "' [" + label + "]: " + runMsg(r))
    say("stderr: " + trim(errText.body))
    return Outcome{ ok: false, statsOk: false, collections: -1, abandoned: -1 }
  }

  let outText = readText(outPath)
  if (!outText.ok) {
    say("stress '" + name + "' [" + label + "]: could not read captured stdout " + outPath)
    return Outcome{ ok: false, statsOk: false, collections: -1, abandoned: -1 }
  }
  if (outText.body != expected) {
    say("stress '" + name + "' [" + label + "]: output mismatch")
    say("  expected: " + trim(expected))
    say("  got:      " + trim(outText.body))
    return Outcome{ ok: false, statsOk: false, collections: -1, abandoned: -1 }
  }

  // Both runs carry `BIT_GC_STATS=1`, so the aggregate line must be there in
  // BOTH. Its absence is the tell that the environment never reached the child
  // (or that the capture is broken), and it is fatal for exactly that reason —
  // unless the program has DECLARED that it owns the exit path and therefore
  // never reaches `statsReport` (`no-stats`, see `checkProgram`).
  if (!statsPresent(errText.body)) {
    if (statsRequired) {
      say("stress '" + name + "' [" + label + "]: no '" + aggregatePrefix + "' line in " + errPath + ".")
      say("  BIT_GC_STATS=1 was set for this run, so the line is missing only if the")
      say("  environment never reached the child or the capture is broken. Either way")
      say("  the collector oracle below would read every field as absent. A program that")
      say("  replaces the runtime's own exit path declares that with a 'no-stats' file.")
      return Outcome{ ok: false, statsOk: false, collections: -1, abandoned: -1 }
    }
    return Outcome{ ok: true, statsOk: false, collections: -1, abandoned: -1 }
  }
  let collections = statsField(errText.body, "collections=")
  let abandoned = statsField(errText.body, "abandoned=")
  if (collections < 0 || abandoned < 0) {
    say("stress '" + name + "' [" + label + "]: malformed stats line in " + errPath)
    return Outcome{ ok: false, statsOk: false, collections: -1, abandoned: -1 }
  }
  return Outcome{ ok: true, statsOk: true, collections: collections, abandoned: abandoned }
}

// Both policies for one program, plus the oracles the second run must satisfy.
// Returns 1 on failure, 0 on success.
function checkProgram(cx: Ctx, name: string, dir: string): Verdict {
  let bad = Verdict{ failed: 1, oracled: false, proved: false }
  let expected = readText(dir + "/" + name + ".expected")
  if (!expected.ok) {
    // A hard error, never a skip: a rename would otherwise delete a program from
    // the corpus in silence.
    say("stress '" + name + "': cannot read " + dir + "/" + name + ".expected")
    return bad
  }

  let noCollect = readMarker(name, dir, "no-collect")
  let noAbandon = readMarker(name, dir, "no-abandon")
  let waived = readMarker(name, dir, "stress-waived")
  // A program that REPLACES the runtime's write/exit provider — `rootiodarwin`
  // and `rootiolinux` are the whole of it — never reaches `statsReport`, so it
  // emits no aggregate line however the environment is set. Declared, with a
  // reason, exactly like the other markers: the alternative is treating a
  // missing line as tolerable everywhere, which is how the oracle stops oracling.
  let noStats = readMarker(name, dir, "no-stats")

  let bin = cx.scratch + "/bin-" + name
  if (!buildProgram(cx, name, dir, bin)) { return bad }

  let base = "default"
  let first = runOnce(cx, name, bin, "a", base, "BIT_GC_STATS=1", expected.body, !noStats.present)
  if (!first.ok) { fsRemove(bin); return bad }

  let label = "BIT_GC=stress"
  let envLine = "BIT_GC=stress BIT_GC_STATS=1"
  if (waived.present) {
    // The waiver admits a hole rather than arguing one away, so it carries its
    // reason into the output on every single run.
    say("stress '" + name + "': STRESS-WAIVED, running BIT_GC=off instead:")
    say("  " + waived.reason)
    label = "BIT_GC=off (waived)"
    envLine = "BIT_GC=off BIT_GC_STATS=1"
  }
  let second = runOnce(cx, name, bin, "b", label, envLine, expected.body, !noStats.present)
  fsRemove(bin)
  if (!second.ok) { return bad }
  if (!second.statsOk) { return Verdict{ failed: 0, oracled: false, proved: false } }

  // `BIT_GC=off` collects nothing BY DEFINITION, so a non-zero count here means
  // the policy in the wrapper is not the policy the child ran under. The waived
  // programs are the only place the port can prove that directly.
  if (waived.present) {
    if (second.collections != 0) {
      say("stress '" + name + "' [" + label + "]: " + num(second.collections) +
        " collections under BIT_GC=off. The policy in the wrapper did not reach the child.")
      return bad
    }
    return Verdict{ failed: 0, oracled: false, proved: false }
  }

  let oracled = !noCollect.present
  let proved = false
  if (oracled) {
    if (second.collections == 0) {
      say("stress '" + name + "' [" + label + "]: the collector never ran, so this run verified")
      say("  nothing about rooting. Give the program a loop where the roots it cares about are")
      say("  live, or - if it proves something about emission or linking rather than rooting -")
      say("  add a 'no-collect' file to its directory stating why.")
      return bad
    }
    // The policy differential, and the reason it is reported rather than
    // enforced HERE. Allocation-driven programs clear `stressRatio` by two to
    // five orders of magnitude, but a sleep-bounded one does not: `schedtimer`
    // measured 99 against 41 and `schedwakedarwin` 150 against 62, because their
    // loops are paced by a clock rather than by back-edges. Failing those two
    // would be calibrating the oracle to the wrong thing. The plumbing claim is
    // a property of the HARNESS, not of each program, so it is asserted once
    // over the corpus in `main` — see `minProofShare`.
    proved = second.collections > first.collections * stressRatio
    if (!proved) {
      say("stress '" + name + "': " + num(second.collections) + " collections under " + label +
        " against " + num(first.collections) + " under the default policy - below the " +
        num(stressRatio) + "x margin, so this program is not one of the corpus's policy proofs.")
    }
  }

  // Opt-in, and only where the program was built so the defect is the sole
  // possible cause (ABI.md §6): an abandoned rendezvous is always SAFE, and a
  // thread asleep in `parkSleepNs` reads as `running`, so most programs here
  // cannot promise zero.
  if (noAbandon.present && second.abandoned != 0) {
    say("stress '" + name + "' [" + label + "]: " + num(second.abandoned) +
      " collections were ABANDONED against " + num(second.collections) + " collected, and this")
    say("  program's 'no-abandon' marker says none may be. Something stayed in the mutator")
    say("  registry as 'running' that should not have - usually an OS thread that exited")
    say("  without its slot being released (ABI.md §6).")
    return bad
  }
  return Verdict{ failed: 0, oracled: oracled, proved: proved }
}

// --- setup ------------------------------------------------------------------

function targetTriple(t: int): string {
  if (t == targetX64Linux) { return "x86_64-linux" }
  if (t == targetArm64Linux) { return "aarch64-linux" }
  if (t == targetArm64Macos) { return "aarch64-macos" }
  die("unknown host target ordinal " + num(t))
  return ""
}

function timeoutMs(): int {
  let raw = trim(osEnv("BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S"))
  if (len(raw) == 0) { return defaultTimeoutS * 1000 }
  let v = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(raw)) {
    if (raw[i] < 48 || raw[i] > 57) { die("BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S is not a number: " + raw) }
    v = v * 10 + int(raw[i]) - 48
    i = i + 1
  }
  if (v == 0) {
    // CLAUDE.md documents 0 as "disable and block forever". `osRunBounded` has no
    // unbounded mode, so say what actually happens instead of pretending.
    say("stress: BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S=0 cannot disable the bound - osRunBounded clamps to one")
    say("  hour (ABI.md §19). Using one hour.")
    return clampTimeoutMs
  }
  return v * 1000
}

// A PRIVATE COPY of the compiler, never `zig-out/bin/bit` itself: a concurrent
// `./make` rewrites that artifact in place and macOS SIGKILLs the exec,
// failing every case with no output at all (#1644).
function privateCompiler(cx: Ctx, src: string): string {
  let dst = cx.scratch + "/bit"
  let r = runScript(cx, cx.scratch + "/copy.sh", "#!/bin/sh\nexec cp " + shQuote(src) + " " + shQuote(dst) + "\n")
  if (r != 0) { die("cannot copy the compiler: " + runMsg(r)) }
  if (fsChmod(dst, 493) != 0) { die("cannot chmod the compiler copy") }
  return dst
}

function main() {
  let root = trim(osEnv("BIT_STRESSGATE_ROOT"))
  if (len(root) == 0) { root = "." }
  if (!fsIsDir(root + "/tests/stress") || !fsIsDir(root + "/stdlib")) {
    die("run this from the repository root (or set BIT_STRESSGATE_ROOT): '" + root +
      "' has no tests/stress + stdlib")
  }

  // The two debugging knobs, and the asymmetry between them is deliberate.
  // `BIT_STRESSGATE_ONLY` narrows which programs are EXECUTED; it does not touch
  // enumeration or the host gate, so BOTH corpus floors still apply under it and
  // a filtered run cannot hide an empty walk or an inverted host gate.
  // `BIT_STRESSGATE_DIR` replaces the corpus outright, which the floors are
  // counting - so that one, and only that one, turns them off and says so.
  let corpus = trim(osEnv("BIT_STRESSGATE_DIR"))
  let only = trim(osEnv("BIT_STRESSGATE_ONLY"))
  let fixture = len(corpus) > 0
  let partial = fixture || len(only) > 0
  if (len(corpus) == 0) { corpus = root + "/tests/stress" }
  if (partial) {
    say("stress: *** PARTIAL RUN - NOT A GATE *** (BIT_STRESSGATE_DIR/ONLY set)")
  }
  if (fixture) { say("stress: corpus floors are OFF - BIT_STRESSGATE_DIR replaced the corpus") }

  let target = hostTarget()
  let compilerSrc = root + "/zig-out/bin/bit"
  let libbitrt = root + "/zig-out/lib/" + targetTriple(target) + "/libbitrt.a"
  if (!fsExists(compilerSrc)) { die("no self-hosted compiler at " + compilerSrc) }
  if (!fsExists(libbitrt)) { die("no runtime archive at " + libbitrt) }

  let scratch = "/tmp/bit-stressgate-" + num(timeUnixNs())
  if (fsMkdir(scratch) != 0) { die("cannot create scratch dir " + scratch) }

  let cx = Ctx{
    root: root,
    compiler: compilerSrc,
    stdlib: root + "/stdlib",
    libbitrt: libbitrt,
    scratch: scratch,
    timeoutMs: timeoutMs(),
    target: target,
  }
  cx = Ctx{
    root: cx.root,
    compiler: privateCompiler(cx, compilerSrc),
    stdlib: cx.stdlib,
    libbitrt: cx.libbitrt,
    scratch: cx.scratch,
    timeoutMs: cx.timeoutMs,
    target: cx.target,
  }

  let names = sortNames(splitNul(fsListDir(corpus)))
  if (len(names) > maxPrograms) { die("more than " + num(maxPrograms) + " entries in " + corpus) }

  let dirs = []string(0)
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(names) && i < maxPrograms) {
    if (fsIsDir(corpus + "/" + names[i])) { dirs = append(dirs, names[i]) }
    i = i + 1
  }
  if (!fixture && len(dirs) < minPrograms) {
    die("found " + num(len(dirs)) + " program directories in " + corpus + ", expected at least " +
      num(minPrograms) + " - the corpus walk found nothing to run")
  }

  // Apply the host gate as its OWN pass, and check its floor before a single
  // program is built. A program that can only run on one OS marks itself:
  // `darwin-only` is `extern function` (SPEC §11.7) — Bit's ELF output is fully
  // static with no dynamic symbol table, so an extern symbol has nothing to
  // resolve against and the compiler rejects it outright — and `linux-only` is
  // the mirror, for `syscall` (§11.8). Neither compiles elsewhere, so it is a
  // skip, not a failure.
  //
  // Separated from the run loop for two reasons. The floor below is the guard
  // against an INVERTED gate, and an inverted gate selects exactly the programs
  // that cannot compile here — so checking it afterwards means sitting through
  // 19 compile failures to be told the gate is backwards. And `eligible` is
  // counted before `ONLY` is applied, so a filtered run still enforces it: a
  // guard a debugging knob can switch off is not a guard.
  let eligible = []string(0)
  let skippedDarwin = 0
  let skippedLinux = 0
  let e = 0
  while (e < len(dirs)) {
    let dir = corpus + "/" + dirs[e]
    if (fsExists(dir + "/darwin-only") && target != targetArm64Macos) {
      skippedDarwin = skippedDarwin + 1
    } else if (fsExists(dir + "/linux-only") && target == targetArm64Macos) {
      skippedLinux = skippedLinux + 1
    } else {
      eligible = append(eligible, dirs[e])
    }
    e = e + 1
  }
  let skipped = skippedDarwin + skippedLinux
  if (!fixture && len(eligible) < minRunnable) {
    die("only " + num(len(eligible)) + " of " + num(len(dirs)) + " programs are runnable on this host (" +
      num(skipped) + " skipped), expected at least " + num(minRunnable))
  }
  // The count above does NOT catch an inverted gate, and finding that out is the
  // reason this check exists. The corpus carries 19 `darwin-only` and 19
  // `linux-only` directories — inverting the comparison SWAPS which 19 are
  // skipped and leaves the runnable total at 59, green, with a skip count that
  // still looks right. Only counting the two marker kinds SEPARATELY breaks that
  // symmetry: on macOS the darwin-only skip count must be zero and the
  // linux-only one must not be, and inverting the gate reverses exactly that.
  let wrongWay = skippedDarwin
  let rightWay = skippedLinux
  if (target != targetArm64Macos) {
    wrongWay = skippedLinux
    rightWay = skippedDarwin
  }
  if (!fixture && (wrongWay != 0 || rightWay < minHostSkips)) {
    die("host gate is inverted: skipped " + num(wrongWay) + " programs marked for THIS host and " +
      num(rightWay) + " marked for the other (expected 0 and at least " + num(minHostSkips) + ")")
  }

  let ran = 0
  let failures = 0
  let oracled = 0
  let proofs = 0
  let k = 0
  while (k < len(eligible)) {
    let name = eligible[k]
    k = k + 1
    if (len(only) > 0 && indexOfAt("," + only + ",", "," + name + ",", 0) < 0) { continue }
    ran = ran + 1
    let v = checkProgram(cx, name, corpus + "/" + name)
    failures = failures + v.failed
    if (v.oracled) { oracled = oracled + 1 }
    if (v.proved) { proofs = proofs + 1 }
  }

  // THE PLUMBING ASSERTION, and it is the one guard the Zig harness could not
  // have. `collections != 0` cannot tell `BIT_GC=stress` from the default run
  // repeated, so if the second wrapper's environment silently stopped arriving
  // every program would still pass its own oracle while precise rooting was
  // never exercised. Collecting at every safepoint costs two to five orders of
  // magnitude, so a majority of the oracled programs must show it. Asserted over
  // the corpus rather than per program because a clock-paced program legitimately
  // cannot clear the margin (see `checkProgram`), and asserted as a SHARE rather
  // than a count so a filtered run enforces it too.
  say("stress: policy proofs " + num(proofs) + "/" + num(oracled) +
    " (stress count above " + num(stressRatio) + "x the default count)")
  if (oracled >= minProofSample && proofs * minProofShare < oracled) {
    say("stress: FAIL - only " + num(proofs) + " of " + num(oracled) + " oracled programs collected")
    say("  meaningfully more under BIT_GC=stress than under the default policy. That is not a")
    say("  stress pass, it is the default run performed twice: the environment in the second")
    say("  wrapper is not reaching the child.")
    failures = failures + 1
  }

  if (failures != 0) {
    say("stress: FAIL - " + num(ran) + " programs run, " + num(skipped) + " skipped, " +
      num(failures) + " failed")
    osExit(1)
  }
  let tag = "PASS"
  if (partial) { tag = "PASS (PARTIAL RUN - NOT A GATE)" }
  say("stress: " + tag + " - " + num(ran) + " programs run, " + num(skipped) + " skipped")
}
